  <section id="chto-eto">
    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve leaves the pelvis next to the anterior superior iliac spine — the bony point at the front of the pelvis — and passes under the inguinal ligament. Here it bends and is easily compressed.</p>
    <NerveMap limb="leg" focus="meralgia" lang="en" />
    <h3>Causes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>tight belts, low-rise jeans, corsets, tool belts;</li>
      <li>excess weight, rapid weight gain, pregnancy;</li>
      <li>long sitting or standing in one position;</li>
      <li>surgery and injuries in the pelvic area.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="simptomy">
    <h2>Symptoms</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>burning, tingling, numbness on the outer and front of the thigh;</li>
      <li>the skin feels "foreign", the touch of clothing is unpleasant;</li>
      <li>worse when walking, standing, extending the hip;</li>
      <li>no leg weakness.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="diagnostika">
    <h2>Diagnosis</h2>
    <p>The diagnosis is usually made on examination. If in doubt — nerve conduction study or nerve ultrasound, a diagnostic block, lumbar and pelvic MRI to rule out other causes.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="lechenie">
    <h2>Treatment</h2>
    <p>Most patients improve without surgery: loose clothing without a tight belt, weight loss, avoiding the long postures that trigger symptoms. Medication is prescribed by a doctor. If symptoms persist, a nerve block is performed.</p>
    <p>Surgery — releasing the nerve under the inguinal ligament — is considered rarely: when marked symptoms persist despite treatment and the diagnosis is confirmed by a block.</p>
  </section>

</Article>
